Z||000005||Raydar||02-04-2001||03:11 PM||||I don't believe a word of it. Thirty years ago, there was probably only 20 years supply remaining. New oil reserves are being discovered on a regular basis, and technology is improving to enable the extraction of more oil from the wells we already know about. <BR>Whether the price will remain reasonable is another question altogether. I believe that supply and demand will ultimately drive the price up to the point that alternative fuels will be more desirable.<BR>Just my $.02. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||12
Z||000006||ChadMan||02-04-2001||10:57 PM||||I had a long talk about this topic with a professor of geography a few years back.  He said there is no danger of the world running out of fossil fuels in our lifetimes, but the price of fossil fuels may rise dramatically in a few decades.  <P>This professor said there are many vast untapped sources of oil located in places like northern Siberia and under the deeper parts of the ocean, and that if gas and oil prices rise sufficiently, it will become profitable to drill there.<P>We may be paying today's equivalent of $3 or $4 a gallon for gasoline in 2022 -- but we will still have gas to burn.<P>Wonder how many of us will still own a Fiero by then?||||reg||1
