Z||000012||bHooper||02-05-2001||06:55 AM||||fellas, eventuallly we are going to run out.  when, whos to say.  in my opinion it would be better to prepare for the worse on this.<P>my "formal" college education was in alternative energies, i sell fabric now, so that tells you how important that major was, when i got out of college 12 years ago.<P>however, at that time, the books were anticipating about 39 years of natural oil reserves left.  factor in improved fuel economy, since then, and the chinese and other 3rd world economy's... that 39 years is probably still about right.<P>however, i would be concerned about OPEC cutting back production, and increasing price.  to me, that means that they may be getting near the end of their supply?!<P>nothing is forever, except herpes, the hivs, and tax's, dont forget that!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000013||kslish||02-05-2001||07:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 007DOUG:<BR><B>Believe VERY LITTLE of what the media tells you.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Exactly, most of this (including college studies) use scare tactics to capture reader's interest.  <P>Even if we do run out, I have one word for you....ETHENOL (corn alcohol), but until gas prices rise enough that Ethenol becomes the cheapest fuel (not to mention refineries would need to be converted from gasoline to Ethenol production) we'll all still be using gas.  Ethenol is also slightly less efficient (about 2 mpg less than what you get with gas).  GM used to produce a Lumina with the 3.1L V6 that could run on Ethenol and you can order Ford Tauruses with Ethenol burning capability right now, but since you can't find pure Ethenol at your corner gas station the demand isn't there for the cars, but the technology is there.  <P>Also, in some areas, if you look at the label on your gas pump when you are fueling up some of you will find that your pumping a mix of 90% gasoline and 10% ethenol into your tank right now anyways.  It's used as a fuel cleaner additive and "filler".  Why only 10% Ethenol?  That is the maximum the world's auto makers have determined can be used in today's production cars with no modifications needed.<P>Look on the bright side of things, if the time ever comes I figure it would cost about $500-$1000 at most to convert a Fiero's engine to be Ethenol capable.  Just need to replace any rubber fuel system parts with synthetic ones (straight Ethenol dissolves rubber) like replacing the injectors, fuel pump and so on if needed and install a new ECM that has recalibrated fuel map programming.  If Ethenol ever becomes the fuel of choice, I'm sure aftermarket companies will come into the fold with conversion kits.  <P>Personally, I wish we were using Ethenol right now, it burns cleaner than gas.<P>Now I'm starting to ramble, can you tell I went to college to be a science teacher?<P>Class dismissed. <P>Ken S.<BR>'86 SC and '87 GT<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Chris McDonald||02-05-2001||08:42 AM||||I think about this subject more and more.  Really, nothing is forever and current consumption is enormous.  I never thought about China before either.  <P>It is my opinion that the fall of things as we know it will be directly linked to fossil fuel.  Not to say it is the end of the world, just then end as we know it.  <P>I am restoring a car right now that will consume copious amounts of fuel per mile when complete.  Just a play toy.  My big joke is that I hope to finish it before fossil fuel becomes extinct.  Our 1995 one ton dually gets about 7 mpg.  It kills to go to the pump.  Virtually everthing we have in our house, at work, or in our wallet is directly related to the transportation or use of fossil fuel.  A permanent disruption in that flow would be a permanent disruption in life as we know it.<P>So, until then---drive hard and drive fast.  Buy a SUV and hurry the process along.  <P>See you guys in the corn fields.  ||||reg||1
